'''''Myriophyllum crispatum''''' is a species of [[water milfoil]] native to [[Australia]].

== Description ==
''Myriophyllum crispatum'' has {{cvt|25–60|cm}} tall stems. The leaves grow in whorls of 5–8. Submerged leaves are thread-like with 12–20 leaflets. Intermediate leaves are thicker with 6–8 short pinnae, transitioning into smooth linear terete forms.<ref name="OrchardAE">{{cite journal |last1=Orchard |first1=A. E. |author1-link=A. E. Orchard |year=1985 |title=''Myriophyllum'' (Haloragaceae) in Australasia. II. The Australian Species |journal=[[Brunonia (journal)|Brunonia]] |volume=8 |pages=211–212 |doi=10.1071/BRU9850173}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=PlantNET - FloraOnline |url=https://plantnet.rbgsyd.nsw.gov.au/cgi-bin/NSWfl.pl?page=nswfl&lvl=sp&name=Myriophyllum~crispatum |access-date=2025-06-09 |website=plantnet.rbgsyd.nsw.gov.au}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Myriophyllum crispatum (Upright water milfoil)|url=https://www.shootgardening.com/plants/myriophyllum-crispatum |access-date=2025-06-09 |website=www.shootgardening.com|quote=M. crispatum is an erect, aquatic or marginal perennial with bright green leaves, ovate where submerged and finely-divided when growing above the water. Tiny, red flowers bloom from the leaf axils in summer.}}</ref>

''Myriophyllum crispatum'' is [[monoecious]]. The male flower has 4 cream to dark brown or purple petals. The female flower lacks petals.<ref name="OrchardAE"/>

== Ecology ==
''Myriophyllum crispatum'' occurs in Western Australia, south-eastern Australia, northern [[Tasmania]], and south-eastern [[Queensland]].<ref name="OrchardAE"/> It is most commonly found in shallow, stagnant water.

